- [Interviewer] Right.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.860
- And then, you know, maybe couple days later,
a week later, hour later, whenever it was,

00:00:54.860 --> 00:01:00.070
I don't know, I got the urge to, no man, I
got to get up, I got to do it again.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:09.760
I don't think that my goal has changed in
the sense of 

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.450
I rap from the same aspect of when I did when
I was a kid like, I just wanted to get the

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.580
respect, I just wanted to get a reaction out
of people, I just wanted to be heard and I

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.380
wanted people to respect what I do and respect
the fact that I respect the craft.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.020
You know what I mean?

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.020
Of them singing.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.770
So I really just kind of, it's pretty much
the same thing today.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.270
Hip-hop has always been about braggin' and
boastin' and I'm better than you with this,

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.590
and I'm better than you with that, and I finally
found something that, yeah, this kid over

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:48.180
here, he may have more chicks, like he may
have better clothes or whatever, but he can't

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.180
do this like me.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.180
You know what I mean?

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.250
He can't write what I'm writing right now.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:57.670
And it started to feel like, you know, maybe
Marshall is getting a little respect.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.740
I think one of my greatest inspirations, or
the things that I would feed off, basically

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:12.950
